  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_NO_SPECS` | `specs/README.md` absent, or no approved scope spec to scaffold |
    | `H_SCOPE_NOT_DECOMPOSED` | Scope type is missing/ambiguous; product/library Module Map is not a non-empty exact manifest of its canonical module specs; or an interface is used as a task owner. Start the nested same-chain module-decomposition correction below, preserve `intent: scaffold` and the exact bounded target-set, then return to scaffold `STEP_0_INTAKE`; infrastructure is the sole flat-scope exception. |
    | `H_AMBIGUOUS_MODE` | Tasks exist + mode verb absent (per `AX_MODE_AUTO_DETECT_OR_HALT`) |
    | `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE` | A rule reference or `<DependsOn>` entry does not resolve to an existing file (`AX_RULES_RESOLUTION_HARD_FAIL`) |
    | `H_RULES_CYCLE` | `<DependsOn>` closure cycles during per-phase rule activation (STEP_3) |
    | `H_VERIFICATION_COMMAND_MISSING` | A `<RequiresVerification>` alias is absent from infra-spec Verification Commands (alias, ticket ID) |
    | `H_BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS_MISSING` | The target scope being scaffolded (the intake target) lacks a Bootstrap Requirements section — checked ONLY for that scope, not for every ✅ scope in the Portal (did not pass the scope flow) |
    | `H_SPEC_SCHEMA_INVALID` | `sdd-state` reports the target scope/module spec as `invalid`; structural evidence is ambiguous and only its owning authoring flow may repair it |
    | `H_ID_COLLISION` | A generated Task-ID / slug collides with an existing one |
    | `H_TYPING_SCENARIO_MISSING` | A DbC contract in Spec References has no `contract`-level typing scenario mapped to a test |
    | `H_TICKET_AUTHORING_INVALID` | One just-filled ticket still fails its bounded single-ticket authoring gate after three repair attempts; no later ticket or index may be created |
    | `H_SCAFFOLD_NOT_EXECUTABLE` | The feasibility critic cannot reach an operator-disposed state within its bounded loop, or a required fresh fallback cannot receive the full latest target-set plus retained context/history |
    | `H_OPERATOR_REJECT` | Operator rejected the breakdown or the test plan |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_0_INTAKE">
      <Goal>Read the Portal; detect scopes, graph, mode. (auto)</Goal>
      <Action>
        Scope names, types, and statuses come from the current portal's Scope Graph + Scopes table
        (`sdd-state`, per `AX_SCOPE_GRAPH_DETECTION`) already in context — the skill that dispatched
        this flow already called it; a fresh `sdd-state` call here is a wasted turn. Read
        `specs/README.md` itself ONLY for the one thing that snapshot does not carry: the
        dependency edges A→B of the Mermaid Scope Graph (absent portal → `H_NO_SPECS`). Before opening
        any scope spec, resolve the target set from the operator/session scaffold payload, then compute
        its transitive `depends-on` closure from that already-bounded portal graph. Read only target +
        closure scope specs and their linked module specs — specs only, never code
        (`AX_SPEC_IS_SOLE_SOURCE`). When the request intentionally means the whole approved project or
        names no narrower target, the explicit bounded fallback is every ✅ scope listed by the same
        `sdd-state` snapshot; say that fallback was selected. Never replace a missing/ambiguous target
        with filesystem discovery, glob/ls, or an all-spec archaeology pass.

        After this exact bounded target set is resolved, and only when the router-proved session is
        already present and compatible, write `.claude/tmp/sdd-scaffold-workset.txt` with one non-empty
        line per exact repo-relative spec in the set, in stable path order. Grammar is exactly
        `<repo-relative-spec-path> — <role> — open`, where `<role>` is exactly one of `scaffold target`
        or `dependency context`; use `scaffold target` for requested scope/module specs and
        `dependency context` only for transitive read context.
        Then run the one ToolCall below exactly once. If session evidence is absent/incompatible, do
        not call `workset`, open a session, or guess: return to the router's session barrier.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="sessionWorkset">npx gennady sdd-session workset --content-file .claude/tmp/sdd-scaffold-workset.txt</ToolCall> Use sessionWorkset before decomposition/DAG work.

        From this bounded set, enforce the decomposition gate fail-closed for each task-owning target:
        scope type must be
        present and unambiguous; product/library requires a non-empty Module Map whose explicit links
        resolve one-to-one to every canonical module spec on disk; interface cannot directly own tasks.
        Otherwise `H_SCOPE_NOT_DECOMPOSED`. Do not issue an extra discovery command for this check.
        Dependency-only interface scopes remain read context, never task owners. Classify every DAG
        node with exactly one task owner: `infrastructure-flat` (scope-level infrastructure),
        `scope-bootstrap` (scope-level bootstrap in a completely decomposed product/library), or
        `module` (ordinary product/library work in one declared module). Product/library ordinary
        work without a module is invalid; interface has no owner. Every `sdd-new task` call repeats
        this typed guard defensively; `--owner` is mandatory.
        Before Gate 1, map every runtime requirement/deliverable to exactly one declared module,
        including composition-root files such as `App`, `main`, `index`, and Vite/runtime entrypoints.
        “Outside modules”, no mapping, or multiple candidate modules → `H_SCOPE_NOT_DECOMPOSED`.
        Invoke `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/module.directive.xml")` as a nested
        same-chain module-decomposition correction: preserve the compatible session's
        `intent: scaffold` and exact bounded target-set, and set its return owner to scaffold
        `STEP_0_INTAKE` without inventing a session field or relabelling intent. An active
        `CHANGE_MANIFEST` under `intent: scaffold` is the resume evidence for this nested owner.
        On pause keep that intent/target-set and record the concrete blocker in `open`; on the nested correction return,
        no ticket or Gate 1 may begin until the corrected decomposition has its owning review disposition;
        re-enter STEP_0_INTAKE only after accepted/CLEAN. Never auto-assign composition to UI
        and never invent a fourth owner. `scope-bootstrap` is legal only for an exact Bootstrap Requirements row
        whose Owner is `this-scope-task`; it cannot carry app-shell or ordinary runtime work.
        `--out` overrides only destination. If a lower-level caller omits `--scope`, the CLI accepts
        only one SCOPE_TYPE-bearing ancestor of that output path; zero/multiple owners fail with the
        exact request to pass `--scope`, and the same decomposition proof still runs.
        Detect mode per `AX_DIRECTIVE_MODE` / `AX_MODE_AUTO_DETECT_OR_HALT`
        — **detection table (directive-specific):**

        | Mode | Detection |
        |---|---|
        | `initial` | No `*.3-tasks.md` exists yet anywhere under the target scope (module / scope index absent) |
        | `extend-dag` | A `*.3-tasks.md` index already exists for the target scope and intake adds tickets to it |
        | — ambiguous | Index(es) exist but intake states neither add-tickets nor any mode verb → `H_AMBIGUOUS_MODE` |

        `extend-dag` reads existing tickets and preserves their IDs
        (`AX_EXTEND_DAG_PRESERVES_EXISTING_IDS`).
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_0B_PREFLIGHT">
      <Goal>Clear schema, FLOW_VERSION, and target-specific authoring readiness before scaffolding.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Use the `SPEC_SCHEMA` / `FLOW_VERSION` / `AUTHORING_SCOPE` / `AUTHORING_SCOPE_DIAG` / `AUTHORING_SCOPE_NEXT` fields from the `sdd-state` snapshot already produced by
        the skill's GATHER step; do not call `sdd-state` or `sdd-task` again here. Size the gate to this
        request's own blast radius (`AX_PREFLIGHT_BLAST_RADIUS_SCOPED`). First match only findings for
        the target scope spec and its declared module specs (a target path absent from the printed
        non-current findings is `current`): `current` → continue; `stale-migratable` → keep the
        compatible session's existing `intent: scaffold` unchanged (no relabel and no new state field),
        keep the literal intake in current context, then
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/reconcile.directive.xml")` in fix mode with the
        literal typed finding from `sdd-state`. Reconcile decides mechanical vs semantic and routes
        every spec change through its owner; this is a nested same-chain preflight subflow. After its
        verified completion re-enter STEP_0B with the same intake. Do not enter STEP_1/STEP_2, edit a spec, read implementation, generate a
        ticket, or add an Ask here. `invalid` → `H_SPEC_SCHEMA_INVALID` with the named path/reason and
        owning authoring-flow next action; do not reinterpret it as migratable. Then apply the remaining
        gates: v1 intersecting legacy task
        layout → `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/migration-v1-v2.directive.xml")`;
        Match the exact requested target scope name. A single-target scaffold consumes only that line;
        an all-scope request requires every requested non-interface line to be `READY=yes`. Any requested
        `READY=no` or `not-applicable` → stop with that same scope's exact DIAG/NEXT. Unrelated red scope
        lines and aggregate `AUTHORING_READY=no` never block a narrower target. `READY=yes` → continue
        even when `EXECUTION_READY=no`, because the declared bootstrap nodes are what scaffold must create.
        Never load readiness setup from scaffold and never infer readiness from another scope.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_1_CASCADE">
      <Goal>Resolve the rules cascade. (auto)</Goal>
      <Action>
        Read the rule registry — `ai/directives/knowledge.xml`, `<Rules>` section. Per scope, resolve
        the effective rule-set per `AX_RULES_CASCADE_RESOLUTION`:
        1. collect the traversed-scopes tier — union of Effective Rules from every scope reachable via
           `depends-on` from the target scope (transitive closure);
        2. overlay the target-scope tier (scope spec Effective Rules for infra; Rules for
           product/library), then each `module:<name>` tier (module spec Handoff Module Rules
           Additions), then task-tier rules the operator supplied — later tier wins on collision;
        3. resolve every rule ref to an existing file (`AX_RULES_RESOLUTION_HARD_FAIL`, else
           `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE`);
        4. build the per-scope Cascade Table (Scope × Categories) destined for
           `specs/<scope>/<scope>.3-tasks.md`.
        No operator stop — mechanical.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_DAG">
      <Goal>Build the acyclic task DAG. — GATE 1: operator approves the BREAKDOWN.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Bootstrap Requirements of the target scope's spec → Phase-0 bootstrap tickets
        (`AX_BOOTSTRAP_TICKET_DERIVATION`); missing section on the target scope →
        `H_BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS_MISSING` (checked only for the scope in intake, not for other
        ✅ scopes reachable via the graph). Form tickets per
        the closed mapping from STEP_0: every runtime node names exactly one declared module owner;
        only exact `this-scope-task` Bootstrap Requirements rows may become `scope-bootstrap` nodes.
        A composition-root/app-shell node with absent, ambiguous, or “outside modules” ownership halts
        with `H_SCOPE_NOT_DECOMPOSED` before presenting Gate 1. Do not assign it to UI by convention.
        Then form tickets per
        `AX_DAG_AND_TICKET_BOUNDARIES` (one per coherent spec unit; subdivide only for parallelism or
        context overflow); cross-scope placement per `AX_CROSS_SCOPE_TASK_PLACEMENT`; assign slugs
        (`AX_TASK_ID_UNIQUENESS`). Acyclic. **Present the DAG (the breakdown) as a decision card and
        get operator approval — this is the one gate on structure.** While awaiting it return
        `TerminalDecision: pause` and preserve the session. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_TASK_GENERATION">
      <Goal>Load the common ticket-authoring contract and order; do not plan ticket bodies. (auto)</Goal>
      <Action>
        Retain only STEP_2's approved node order, shared cascade facts, and shared test/toolchain
        facts. Do NOT form, draft, or retain any node's ticket content here; content belongs to the
        current-node iteration in STEP_3_TICKET_LOOP, after every prior node is green. Unresolved
        coverage/toolchain alternatives go to STEP_4. The per-node contract is
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/task-ticket-structure.xml")`. Per
        current ticket: decompose into phases (`AX_PHASES_DECLARED_IN_HEADER`; impl and test always separate).
        Fill every phase's `Deleted Files:` with exact tracked paths the phase removes, or the literal
        `none`; this is mechanical DAG data and adds no operator interaction. Deleted paths do not belong in `Target Files`.
        For each readiness Bootstrap Requirement, copy its exact `Readiness Gates` only into the
        single phase that creates every listed `Gate Artifact`; include those artifacts in that
        phase's `Target Files`. Ordinary phases omit `Readiness Gates`. Activate rules per phase
        (`AX_RULE_ACTIVATION_PLAN`):
        1. candidates = the scope's cascade set from STEP_1;
        2. include each candidate whose `<Triggers>` match THIS phase's `Target Files` + `kind` and
           whose `<SkipWhen>` does not apply;
        3. close the set over `<DependsOn>` transitively — open each included rule file, add every
           dependency, recurse until the set stops growing (cycle → `H_RULES_CYCLE`; missing file →
           `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE`) — the list ships fully resolved, the phase-subagent never walks
           `<DependsOn>` at runtime;
        4. write the phase `Rules:` bullet list — one `[<rule-id>](<rule-file-path>)` per rule, direct
           + transitive, all explicit;
        4b. when a DIFFERENT bootstrap ticket owns a shared infra file this phase reads/extends,
            write `владелец — <ticket>` in the phase; never make the worker grep siblings;
        4c. before any generator/scaffolder runs, put every expected created/modified file in
            `Target Files`, with owning spec/module and production/test/tool-config/vendor kind.
            Unknown/version-sensitive output → verify now, never expand the boundary at execution;
        4d. for every impl/refactor phase, fill its `Entities:` field with the exact
            module-Entity-Inventory names this phase implements (verbatim, comma-separated) — this is
            the STRUCTURAL, filename-independent ownership record a `Deferred Implementation: <ticket>`
            marker resolves against (`ticketOwnsEntity`), so it MUST be set whenever another module's
            inventory row defers an entity to this ticket; a file name alone (e.g. `foo-service.ts`
            for `FooService`) does NOT establish ownership. Omit only for test/config/doc phases;
        5. write the ticket Verification table. It lists ONLY what §5 adds ON TOP of the phase
           verify call — never `sdd-verify` itself. STEP_5 of the phase protocol already runs
           <ToolLiteral role="ticket">npx gennady sdd-verify --task <ticket-path> --phase <PhaseID></ToolLiteral> as its own first action; writing that same
           call into the table would make the command recursively repeat its own ladder. The CLI
           executes applicable §5 rows verbatim and owns their receipt. So: no row for
           it. Instead, write one line directly above the table, verbatim:
           `Гейт фазы — <ToolLiteral role="ticket">npx gennady sdd-verify --task <ticket-path> --phase <PhaseID></ToolLiteral> — выполняется
           на STEP_5; профиль, Target Files и owning spec выводятся из тикета; в таблицу не
           дублируется.` Then the table itself: union any OTHER
           `<RequiresVerification>` aliases across the ticket's phases beyond the standard ladder,
           EXCLUDING the coverage-reader alias owned solely by 5b; group the remaining aliases and
           resolve each unique alias from
           infra-spec Verification Commands (absent → `H_VERIFICATION_COMMAND_MISSING`) — one row
           per unique alias, Required-by = comma-separated declaring rule IDs, Role = `extra`.
           Serialize exact Command as a code span with N+1 outer backticks, where N is the longest
           inner backtick run (0 if none); change no inner byte. Parser strips only that wrapper,
           restoring runtime bytes. Raw outside `|` splits cells; never XML-escape.
        5b. COVERAGE POLICY — fill the structured v1 fields from behavior semantics, never from file
           extension, path, phase kind, platform guess, or a default threshold. Exactly one state:
           - `required`: the relevant infra/platform Verification Commands contract MUST provide one
             concrete read/check command. Serialize it by the same exact code-span rule into one
             table row with Role=`coverage`; after the phase DAG exists, record exactly one test phase as
             `Coverage Owner Phase` and set Required-by to one of that phase's rules. Its verifier is
             the single producer; other test phases use ordinary tests. Select a unique owner
             mechanically; otherwise record the exact alternatives for Gate 2, never prompt here. Do not invent
             `testcov`, `80`, target paths, or another platform. The row only reads/checks its report and
             MUST NOT rerun the suite. Missing command owner is
             `H_VERIFICATION_COMMAND_MISSING`; unresolved concrete toolchain alternatives go to Gate 2,
             never a separate prompt here.
           - `not-applicable`: write a concise `Coverage Reason` tied to the ticket behavior and NO
             Coverage Owner Phase or Role=`coverage` row. Config/doc/bootstrap/infra labels alone do not decide this state.
           Missing/conflicting policy is invalid. With no extra aliases, keep the three-column table
           and one `| — | — | extra |` row; never omit the policy fields.
        Draft BDD with the mandatory `contract`-level
        typing scenario per contract (`AX_TICKET_HAS_BDD_AND_TESTS`) and, per `AX_E2E_FIRST`, an
        `e2e`-level scenario for the ticket's user-facing path by default — dropping it needs a
        named Decision Log entry with a risk line, not a quiet "unit is enough" call by the phase
        subagent; pre-fill the Execution Log plan
        (`AX_EXECUTION_LOG_PLAN_VS_FACT`). Error-path and edge-case scenarios are drafted NOW, at this
        step, not left for a later pass (`AX_RELEASE_READY_DEFAULT` — release-ready is the default
        depth unless the operator named POC/MVP); an error scenario asserts against the thrown error
        class + a stable message fragment (`AX_FOCUSED_ERROR_ASSERTIONS`), never a `Result` shape,
        since the canon public-API error mechanism is throw + try/catch (`AX_RESULT_PUBLIC_BOUNDARY` —
        Result is a narrow, contract-scoped exception, not the default). **BDD coverage pass — dispatch
        ONE isolated subagent** with a
        clean slice (module spec + contract anchors + requirements + draft BDD) asking «which negative /
        corner / trust-boundary / failure-mode scenarios are missing?»; merge approved ones — never
        defer one to "later" without an explicit operator decision and a Decision Log entry naming the
        risk (`AX_RELEASE_READY_DEFAULT`) — log the
        reviewed checklist in the ticket Decision Log (`AX_DECISION_LOG_REQUIRED`,
        `DECISION_LOG_ENTRY_FORMAT`). Validate every contract has its typing scenario
        mapped to a test (else `H_TYPING_SCENARIO_MISSING`). Check each emitted ticket against
        `TASK_TICKET_STRUCTURE`: section anchors match the normative names exactly
        (`AX_TICKET_SECTION_NAMES_NORMATIVE`), and contract material is linked by spec anchor with zero
        copied contract text (`AX_SSOT_TRACEABILITY`) — write every Meta `Spec References:` entry and
        every phase `Spec Refs:` entry in the RESOLVABLE form `<path>#<anchor>`, where `<anchor>` is
        either a `SECTION_NAME` the spec file wraps in `<!--SECTION:NAME-->` markers, or the
        GitHub-style heading slug (lowercase, hyphen-separated) `sdd-extract` resolves directly — e.g.
        `../greeting/greeting.spec.md#module-contracts` for the `## Module Contracts` heading, or
        `../greeting/greeting.spec.md#MODULE_CONTRACTS` when that section carries an explicit anchor.
        A bare prose reference, heading paraphrase, or line number fails
        `sdd-task --phase`'s downstream read at execution time; a link a worker or auditor cannot
        resolve with `sdd-extract <file>#<anchor>` verbatim is not a valid Spec Reference. No operator
        stop — automated.
        Pass only the ordered node identities plus shared facts to STEP_3_TICKET_LOOP; create no
        ticket content, files, or indexes here.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_TICKET_LOOP">
      <Goal>Materialize and validate exactly one prepared ticket at a time. (auto)</Goal>
      <Action>
        Select only the next unprocessed STEP_2 node. Do not inspect or plan the following node yet.
        Run exactly one matching ToolCall below, never a shell loop. From the returned manifest plus
        the common contract, form and fill content only for this current node.
        Copy its returned path-aware owning-spec/rule/deferred literals verbatim into the prepared
        content; never compute paths or translate IDs. Fill that one returned manifest completely.
        Inputs <owner>/<scope>/<module>/<ACR>/<slug>: STEP_2 node. This table is exhaustive; interface maps to none.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="flatInfraTaskManifest">npx gennady sdd-new task --owner infrastructure-flat --scope <scope> --id <ACR>-<slug></ToolCall> Use flatInfraTaskManifest only for infrastructure-flat.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="scopeBootstrapTaskManifest">npx gennady sdd-new task --owner scope-bootstrap --scope <scope> --id <ACR>-<slug></ToolCall> Use scopeBootstrapTaskManifest only for product/library scope-bootstrap.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="moduleTaskManifest">npx gennady sdd-new task --owner module --scope <scope> --module <module> --id <ACR>-<slug></ToolCall> Use moduleTaskManifest only for product/library module.
        Set <created-ticket-path> to the exact ticket path returned by that node's selected manifest.
        Immediately after filling ONE created ticket, run its authoringGate ToolCall below. Red: fix
        only that ticket from the copy-ready findings and repeat the SAME call, at most three attempts;
        then `H_TICKET_AUTHORING_INVALID`. Green: only then may the next node's content be formed.
        Never plan or create a later ticket or any task index while this gate is red; no shell loop
        or command chain.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="authoringGate">npx gennady sdd-check --task <created-ticket-path> --authoring</ToolCall> Use authoringGate before the next ticket.

        After every node has a green per-ticket authoring gate, run the complete-graph feasibilityGate
        exactly once before STEP_3B. It evaluates the materialized graph against clean HEAD and emits
        named causal diagnostics: one exact owner for `package.json` and the active lockfile,
        provider→consumer ordering, and command
        proof. Red: repair only the named ticket fields/edges and rerun the same command; do not dispatch
        the semantic critic. Green: continue to STEP_3B. Never replace these named causal diagnostics with general critic prose.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="feasibilityGate">npx gennady sdd-check --scaffold-feasibility</ToolCall> Use feasibilityGate before STEP_3B_FEASIBILITY_CRITIC.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3B_FEASIBILITY_CRITIC">
      <Goal>Reject structurally valid but operationally impossible tickets before execute. (auto)</Goal>
      <Action>
        First fold the matching persisted `FeasibilityState`. A pending `sensor-result` resumes its
        first incomplete transition below: re-present a required unresolved Ask from that result, or
        apply its recorded disposition, without dispatching, incrementing, or resetting. Only when no
        result is pending may this step dispatch ONE isolated critic with the materialized tickets, owning spec anchors,
        infra Verification Commands, and resolved phase Rules. For every phase, simulate:
        `dependency outputs + phase Target Files → one mandatory phase command (ladder + applicable §5 rows)`.
        The critic must report: a gate that needs a file owned by a later phase; a repository-wide
        fix before ignore/exclusion files exist; shared-file double ownership; an undeclared
        generator output; production entities placed in a scope without module inventory; or a test
        mapping whose code cannot exist yet; or any runtime deliverable (including App/main/index/Vite
        composition roots) without exactly one declared module owner, especially one hidden inside a
        `scope-bootstrap` node without an exact Bootstrap Requirements row.

        Persist and fold one explicit `FeasibilityState` through Gate 2 via
        `FEASIBILITY_STATE_FORMAT`; never rely on chat memory:
        `LatestTargetSet` = every materialized ticket path plus its exact latest bytes and content hash;
        `ChangedTickets` = tickets changed since the last critic result;
        `WorkerSession` = retained id and `alive|unsupported|lost` availability;
        `FallbackUsed` = boolean; `ResultCount` = cumulative critic results; `ActiveCap` = 5 or the
        operator-raised value; `ApprovedGate2Choices` = exact prior approvals;
        `PendingGate2Delta` = only a genuinely new product/toolchain fork;
        `LastResult` = literal verdict plus literal Changes. Before the first critic dispatch append
        the `opened` event. Never reset this record on Gate 2 or fallback.

        Process every critic result in this order, with no shortcut:

        | Event after a result | Mandatory transition |
        |---|---|
        | Any result | Increment `ResultCount`; append and require the complete `sensor-result` event; refresh `LatestTargetSet` and `ChangedTickets`; only then evaluate `ResultCount >= ActiveCap` BEFORE GOAL, Ask, CLEAN, fork, proceed, or re-dispatch. |
        | Active cap reached | Ask the operator per `AX_CAP_5`, regardless of verdict. If Changes is empty, offer `CLEAN`, `CONTINUE THROUGH ROUND N` with an explicit higher cap, or `RESTART: reason`; if Changes is non-empty, omit CLEAN. No automatic next result exists. |
        | Below cap + Changes non-empty | The latest bytes are unreviewed: re-dispatch; ignore any simultaneous CLEAN/fork as stale until a later no-change result reviews those bytes. |
        | Below cap + no Changes + CLEAN | The latest bytes are reviewed; proceed to STEP_4. |
        | Below cap + no Changes + new fork | Set only that fork in `PendingGate2Delta`; proceed to STEP_4 without approving it. |
        | Below cap + other no-change verdict | Apply only its ordinary ticket corrections, refresh `ChangedTickets`, and re-dispatch; unresolved/non-actionable output halts with `H_SCAFFOLD_NOT_EXECUTABLE`. |

        No cap/GOAL Ask is legal until the result/count event is durable. The Ask itself never
        mutates or replaces that event. Append the operator answer as its own immutable
        `operator-disposition` referencing the pending result before applying it. On resume, a
        pending result without that disposition re-presents the same Ask and performs no dispatch.

        Cap disposition is explicit: `CLEAN` accepts the latest no-change bytes and advances;
        `CONTINUE THROUGH ROUND N` sets `ActiveCap=N` greater than `ResultCount`, then evaluates the
        SAME `LastResult` through the below-cap rows without incrementing it twice; `RESTART: reason`
        follows that exact operator reason and is the only disposition allowed to replace this state.

        Re-dispatch into the SAME worker session whenever it is alive; only that live same-session
        path may send just `ChangedTickets`, because the session retains the unchanged target context.
        If continuation is unsupported or the retained session is lost, allow exactly ONE fresh
        fallback critic, mark `FallbackUsed`, and send the FULL `LatestTargetSet` plus the full retained
        state/context and complete prior result history; keep `ResultCount`. If that exact payload
        cannot be delivered, or a second fallback would be required, pause with a teaching
        `H_SCAFFOLD_NOT_EXECUTABLE` blocker that names the missing set/context. Never regenerate the DAG
        or all tickets, never reset the cap, and never hand off unreviewed latest bytes.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="feasibilityEvent">npx gennady sdd-session log --content-file .claude/tmp/sdd-scaffold-feasibility-event.json</ToolCall> Payload: current opened/target-refreshed/worker-state/sensor-result/operator-disposition event. Use feasibilityEvent and require success before its transition.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4_TEST_PLAN_REVIEW">
      <Goal>GATE 2: operator approves the TEST PLAN.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Present the test plan as a decision card: each BDD scenario as a use-case, tagged
        `[<ACRONYM>-REQ-N]`, traced up to the scope Vision / goal it protects, plus coverage (every
        contract has a typing scenario, every scenario maps to a test). The operator approves **what is
        tested, which use-cases are covered, and that this guarantees the vision** — the one gate on
        substance. Include every STEP_3 coverage-owner/toolchain/e2e alternative in this SAME card;
        its one approval resolves all. Out-of-scope proposals the subagent surfaced are raised here.
        Return `TerminalDecision: pause` and preserve the session while stopped for that decision.

        On approval, apply each newly selected coverage owner, verification toolchain, and e2e inclusion or
        explicit waiver directly to the already-generated tickets whose bytes change, including their
        Coverage Policy/Owner Phase, Verification row, BDD mapping, and Decision Log where applicable.
        Append those exact answers to `ApprovedGate2Choices`. Do not ask again about choices already
        recorded as approved; a later card contains only
        `PendingGate2Delta` and its affected use-cases.
        Do not regenerate the DAG, rerun STEP_3 for all tickets, or create a second standing approval
        gate. Refresh `LatestTargetSet` and `ChangedTickets`, clear the materialized
        `PendingGate2Delta`, and use STEP_3B's exact dispatch/fallback transition. A live retained
        session receives only `ChangedTickets`; a first fallback receives the FULL latest target-set,
        retained state/context, and complete result history. Each returned result enters STEP_3B's
        mandatory `increment → cap check → verdict/Changes` transition; therefore result 5 or an
        operator-raised cap always pauses for `AX_CAP_5` even when it says CLEAN or exposes a new fork.
        Only a below-cap, no-change CLEAN over the latest bytes continues to STEP_5. Apply ordinary
        structural corrections autonomously to the affected tickets and recheck within that same
        cumulative budget.

        If that recheck alone exposes a genuinely new product/toolchain fork, return one delta-only
        Gate 2 decision card containing only `PendingGate2Delta` and affected use-cases; do not replay
        already approved choices. After that answer, materialize the delta and use the same state-machine
        transition above. This is a continuation of Gate 2, not another standing gate. A fork result at
        the active cap is disposed by `AX_CAP_5` BEFORE any delta card; it cannot authorize round 6.
        At any cap, return `TerminalDecision: pause`; the persisted event fold is the preserved
        `FeasibilityState`, and unreviewed changed bytes never finalize.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="feasibilityGate2Event">npx gennady sdd-session log --content-file .claude/tmp/sdd-scaffold-feasibility-event.json</ToolCall> Payload: current gate2-choice/target-refreshed/operator-disposition event. Use feasibilityGate2Event before its transition.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_5_FINALIZE">
      <Goal>Write the index hierarchy; hand off. (auto)</Goal>
      <Action>
        For each `<module>.3-tasks.md` that does not exist yet, run moduleIndexManifest ToolCall below
        to lay down the skeleton (nested `--module foo/bar` per
        `AX_HIERARCHICAL_SPECS`); an existing file is filled in place, never re-scaffolded. Fill per
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/module-tasks-index.xml")` (Tracker, Slug
        Registry, intra-module DAG, shared content per `AX_TICKET_DEDUPLICATION`). Roll up the scope
        tracker the same way — scopeIndexManifest ToolCall below when `<scope>.3-tasks.md` is
        missing, then fill per
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/scope-tasks-index.xml")` — and roll up
        the project index the same way — projectIndexManifest ToolCall below when `specs/3-tasks.md` is missing,
        then fill per
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/project-tasks-index.xml")`; never a
        manual `Write` of `specs/3-tasks.md` from scratch.
        Then run integrityCheck ToolCall below (task-DAG: id-collisions · deps resolve · acyclic; tracker↔ticket sync;
        phase graph; exec-log) and resolve or consciously defer every finding per
        `AX_CLOSE_WITH_INTEGRITY_CHECK` — the scaffold mutation is done only once the task graph passes.
        `BDD_COVERAGE` (canonical case names exist in the declared test file) gates on the ticket's
        own Meta Status — the existence check runs only once that ticket is `[x] DONE`, never for a
        freshly-scaffolded `[ ] TODO` ticket (whose test files do not exist yet by design), so a clean
        integrityCheck ToolCall below right after this step is expected, not a sign the check is silently
        skipped. Fix only structural findings at this step (id collisions, unresolved deps, cycles,
        tracker drift, broken `](…spec.md)` links) — a BDD/test-existence finding here would mean a
        ticket was mis-marked DONE before its tests exist, a scaffold defect worth fixing on its own,
        not phase noise to suppress.
        These trackers and tickets are read by execute with no memory of this scaffolding run —
        every DAG edge, rule reference, and BDD scenario must already be in the file
        (`AX_STATELESS_ARTIFACT`). Emit a created-files summary with traceability links. Hand off to
        execute in the SAME session —
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/execute.directive.xml")` — no operator stop;
        isolation lives one level down, at the phase-worker subagent execute dispatches per phase, not
        at this handoff. After integrityCheck is clean, append the exact `closed` feasibility event;
        its successful content-file call consumes the payload and closes this persisted cycle. Return
        `TerminalDecision: continue`; scaffold is an intermediate owner and
        never runs `sdd-session close`.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="moduleIndexManifest">npx gennady sdd-new module-index --scope <scope> --module <module></ToolCall> Use moduleIndexManifest.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="scopeIndexManifest">npx gennady sdd-new scope-index --scope <scope></ToolCall> Use scopeIndexManifest.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="projectIndexManifest">npx gennady sdd-new project-index</ToolCall> Use projectIndexManifest.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="integrityCheck">npx gennady sdd-check --all .</ToolCall> Use integrityCheck.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="feasibilityClosedEvent">npx gennady sdd-session log --content-file .claude/tmp/sdd-scaffold-feasibility-event.json</ToolCall> Payload: exact closed event. Use feasibilityClosedEvent after integrityCheck succeeds.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>

